    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the value of \\(a\\) so that the lines given by the equations \\(y = 2x+13\\) and \\(6y + ax = 6\\) are perpendicular, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the slope of the first line.\n2. Use the formula for the slope between two perpendicular lines to find the slope of the second line.\n3. Find the value of \\(a\\) that makes the slopes equal.\n\nLet's solve this step-by-step.\n\n### Step 1: Find the slope of the first line.\n\nThe first line is given by the equation:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\nFrom this line, we can directly read the slope as:\n\\[ \\text{slope} = 2 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Find the slope of the second line.\n\nThe second line is given by the equation:\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\nConvert this equation to slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\) to find the slope. \n\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\n\nFrom this, we can directly read the slope as:\n\\[ \\text{slope} = -\\frac{a}{6} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the value of \\(a\\).\n\nTwo lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\). So we set the product of the slopes equal to \\(-1\\) and solve for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1 \\]\n\nNow we will solve this equation step-by-step using Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variable\na = symbols('a')\n\n# Define the equation for the perpendicular slopes\nequation = Eq(2 * (-a/6), -1)\n\n# Solve the equation for a\nsolution = solve(equation, a)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines \\(y = 2x+13\\) and \\(6y + ax = 6\\) perpendicular is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
